{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793812714,"nonce":"MAHVV6/539yUuQYH4cZwILqOE0s=","title":"Lua late-resume integration test depends on compiler scheduling","message":"The release resume_points test a_region_outlined_after_the_promotion_gets_a_late_resume_point failed once during the full local validation run. The program returned the correct 1804062937 and transferred to LLVM, but both f regions were outlined before the queued LLVM work completed. Consequently the trace correctly contained ordinary outlined resume points rather than the exact late resume point (llvm) string the test requires. The first LLVM install took 410.99 ms in that run.\n\nAn isolated rerun of all five resume_points tests passed. Three alternating direct fixture runs per arm, using the pre-change local binary and allocation-provenance candidate, all produced the intended late resume point, LLVM transfer, and correct result. This does not establish a deterministic compiler regression; the fixture needs a synchronization mechanism to guarantee its intended ordering rather than relying on relative loop and background-compile durations. Do not weaken the assertion to accept an ordinary early-created region, since that would stop testing late publication.\n\nEvidence: /private/tmp/zyntax-osr-provenance/lua-tests.log, resume-retry.log, late-results.json, and late-{base,candidate}-{0,1,2}.trace. The full TypeScript corpus separately has no regressions. Related resolved runtime bug: 8a22754 (late resume point missing a body).","files":null}]}